Dealing with Missing Data & Stray Characters in Python
This chapter covers essential data preprocessing techniques using pandas to clean datasets, handle missing values, deal with formatting inconsistencies (such as currency symbols and commas), and streamline operations using method chaining.

1. Handling Missing Values
Missing data is common in real-world datasets. Depending on your use case, you can either drop incomplete rows/columns or fill them with constants.

Identifying Missing Values
df.isna() / df.isnull(): Returns a boolean DataFrame showing True where values are missing and False otherwise.

df.notna() / df.notnull(): Returns True for valid (non-missing) entries.

Listwise Deletion (Dropping Missing Data)
Drop all rows with any missing value:

Python
df_clean_rows = df.dropna()
Drop all columns with any missing value:

Python
df_clean_cols = df.dropna(axis=1)
Drop rows where a specific column is missing:

Python
df_subset = df.dropna(subset=['ColumnName'])
Counting Categories (Including Missing Values)
To include missing values (NaN) in value counts:

Python
counts = df['ColumnName'].value_counts(dropna=False)
2. Dealing with Stray Characters
Real-world numerical columns often contain currency symbols ($, £), commas (,), or other formatting characters that prevent them from being cast as numeric types.

Step-by-Step Cleaning
Identify problematic characters by attempting conversion with coercion:

Python
numeric_vals = pd.to_numeric(df['RawSalary'], errors='coerce')
idx = numeric_vals.isna()
print(df['RawSalary'][idx])  # Inspect rows causing issues
Remove unwanted characters using string methods:

Python
df['RawSalary'] = df['RawSalary'].str.replace(',', '', regex=False)
df['RawSalary'] = df['RawSalary'].str.replace('$', '', regex=False)
df['RawSalary'] = df['RawSalary'].str.replace('£', '', regex=False)
Cast to numeric type:

Python
df['RawSalary'] = df['RawSalary'].astype(float)
3. Method Chaining
Instead of writing multiple sequential assignments, you can string together multiple .str methods and type-casting operations in a clean, readable continuous block using method chaining:

Python
df['RawSalary'] = (
    df['RawSalary']
    .str.replace(',', '', regex=False)
    .str.replace('$', '', regex=False)
    .str.replace('£', '', regex=False)
    .astype(float)
)
Benefits of Method Chaining:
Readability: Keeps related transformations grouped together.

Conciseness: Reduces the need for intermediate variables.

Maintainability: Makes it easier to add, remove, or reorder preprocessing steps.
